{
  "requirement": "{cite}`requirements.sdk.graph.criteria.criterion_trait`\n\n`Criterion::version` identifies the version of the review rules and the\ncompleted answer's encoding:\n\n```rust,ignore\npub trait Criterion<D: Domain>: Send + Sized {\n    // ...\n\n    /// Version of these review rules and the result's encoding.\n    /// Change it when an old answer should no longer be reused.\n    fn version(&self) -> CriterionVersion;\n\n    // ...\n}\n```\n\nThe {term}`requirements.sdk.graph.criteria.source_node`,\n{term}`requirements.sdk.graph.criteria.question_path`, and version identify\nthe same question for checking and execution. Parameters that change which\nquestion is being asked belong in its identity or version.\n\n[Storage](evidence.md#identify-the-saved-assessment) explains how the source node\nand criterion path identify a saved answer and how its version is recorded.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The subject has one supplied test, `crates/trust-but-verify/src/graph/validation_tests.rs#fn[caller_reuses_negative_answers_and_compares_revisions_explicitly]`. It is marked `{test}` for `requirements.sdk.graph.criteria.criterion_trait.version`. I found no other relevant test that lacks a marker.\n\nThe test's `Review` criterion (`impl[Review/Criterion<Memory>]`) returns `CriterionVersion::new(self.revision)` from `version`. It has an empty `criterion_path` and takes its source node from `node`.\n\nObligations and the test scenarios that cover them:\n\n1. **`version` identifies the version of the review rules and the answer's encoding.**\n   - After `store_evidence`, `load_evidence` of a `v1` review returns a cached answer.\n   - The test asserts that `review().version().is_compatible_with(cached.version())` holds, so the stored version matches the criterion's version.\n   - After storing the `v2` replacement, it asserts that the record's stored version `as_str()` is `\"v2\"`. This shows the criterion's version is what gets recorded.\n   - It asserts that the loaded old record's version is `\"v1\"`.\n\n2. **A changed version makes an old answer no longer reusable.**\n   - A `v2` review loads the `v1` record for the same node and path.\n   - The test asserts `!v2.version().is_compatible_with(old.version())`, so a changed version means the old answer is not compatible. It then re-verifies and stores the replacement.\n\n3. **The source node, criterion path and version together identify the same question.**\n   - A review of a different node (8) with the same version finds no evidence, so the source node is part of the identity.\n   - The same node, path and version finds the stored answer, so identical identity is reused.\n   - A changed version on the same node and path is detected as incompatible, which shows the version is part of the identity.\n   - A record with no stored version (`version = None`) is compatible with `CriterionVersion::new(\"v2\")`. The compatibility check handles this case explicitly.\n\n4. **Parameters that change the question belong in the identity or version.**\n   - This is design guidance, not a separately checkable behavior. The `Review` criterion carries its `revision` parameter in the version, and the test shows that changing it is detected.\n\nThe assertions check the expected outcomes rather than only running the code. I found no gap and no marking gap, and the subject has no acknowledgments.",
  "references": "9d1262279cbc6fdf48f62348b85ccec06e728abcb77450a3b226ffc50df6772f",
